= Solution
{parent=c}

A lambda term $F$ lambda-defines $f:\mathbb N^k\to\mathbb N$ when, for all $n_1,\ldots,n_k$,
$$
F\,c_{n_1}\cdots c_{n_k}\equiv_\beta c_{f(n_1,\ldots,n_k)},
$$
where $c_n$ is the <Church numeral> for $n$.

= d
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

No. Let $c_0=\lambda f.\lambda x.x$ and set
$$
R=\lambda n.c_0,
\qquad
S=\lambda n.n\,(\lambda x.c_0)\,c_0.
$$
Both terms send every <Church numeral> to $c_0$, so both define the constant-zero function. They are distinct beta-normal forms, however, and the <Church-Rosser theorem> implies that distinct beta-normal forms cannot be beta-equivalent.

= e
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Take
$$
\delta=\lambda x.\lambda f.f(xf).
$$
If $L$ is a <fixed-point combinator>, then $Lf\equiv_\beta f(Lf)$, so eta-conversion gives
$$
L\equiv_\eta\lambda f.Lf\equiv_\beta\lambda f.f(Lf)=\delta L.
$$
Conversely, if $L\equiv_{\beta\eta}\delta L$, application to an arbitrary $f$ gives $Lf\equiv_{\beta\eta}f(Lf)$, which is precisely the fixed-point-combinator property.

